    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Tekton built its reputation by doing a few unglamorous things well: complete size runs with no missing sockets, sensible organizers instead of blow-molded cases, clean chrome, and a no-receipt warranty on its hand tools. For a home mechanic it is one of the best values in the business. It is not a Snap-on replacement in every drawer, and a few product lines are average, but the core sockets and wrenches are good enough that many professionals keep them as their second set.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Verdict Up Front
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton is one of the best choices for a home mechanic or DIYer building a hand-tool kit. Sockets, combination wrenches and organizer-based sets offer professional-looking quality for a fraction of what the truck brands charge, and the warranty on hand tools is about as simple as warranties get.
          </p>

          <p>
            For professionals, Tekton works well as a secondary or specialty set, and many mechanics use Tekton sockets and wrenches daily without trouble. Where it falls short of premium brands is in fine details: ratchet mechanisms that are good but not class-leading, finishes that are clean but not the thinnest-walled, and a narrower range of specialty tools.
          </p>

          <p>
            The things to buy are sockets, wrenches, screwdrivers, pliers, and Tekton's click torque wrenches, which are a strong value. The things to compare carefully are ratchets, where GearWrench and the truck brands offer finer mechanisms, and specialty automotive tools, where Tekton's range is thinner.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tekton is a hand-tool brand only. For power tools to go with it, see our <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt comparison</a> or the budget-focused <a href="/ryobi-tools-review/">Ryobi tools review</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Category</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Verdict</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sockets and socket sets</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent value, complete size runs</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Combination wrenches</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Very good for the price</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ratchets</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good, but not class-leading</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Click torque wrenches</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strong value with calibration certificate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Screwdrivers and pliers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Solid everyday quality</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Specialty automotive tools</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Limited range compared with others</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="company" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Tekton Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton is a Michigan-based hand-tool company that sells primarily online, through its own website and through Amazon. It does not sell through tool trucks or most big-box stores, which keeps prices lower and lets the company sell direct to customers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most Tekton tools are manufactured in Taiwan and China, as is true for most mid-priced hand-tool brands, including many sold under familiar American names. Taiwanese hand-tool factories in particular are known for producing high-quality tools for many well-known brands. If country of origin matters to you, check the product listing, which usually states it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tekton's design approach emphasizes complete sets. Its socket and wrench sets tend to include every size in a range, rather than skipping sizes that cheaper sets leave out to cut cost. Its organizers favor rails, trays and foam-friendly designs instead of the blow-molded cases that come with many budget sets and rarely last.
          </p>

          <p>
            The company also sells modular storage and builds sets that fit together, so you can add pieces over time without ending up with mismatched cases. That makes it easier to grow a kit as needs change without starting over.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHY IT MATTERS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">No missing sizes saves you a trip to the store.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A set that skips 15 mm or 17 mm will leave you stuck halfway through a brake job. Complete size runs are one of Tekton's most practical strengths.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="sockets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Sockets and Socket Sets
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sockets are Tekton's strongest category. Its hand sockets are chrome vanadium with a polished finish, and most sets use 6-point designs, which grip the flats of a fastener rather than the corners. That reduces rounding on stubborn or slightly corroded bolts, which is exactly what a home mechanic runs into.
          </p>

          <p>
            Size markings are large and easy to read. Tekton sockets are typically laser-etched or stamped clearly, and many sets use color-coded or labeled rails. That sounds minor until you are reaching for a 10 mm socket in a dim engine bay.
          </p>

          <p>
            Impact sockets are made from chrome molybdenum steel with a black finish, designed for use with impact wrenches. Never use chrome hand sockets on an impact wrench; they are harder and more brittle, and they can crack. Tekton sells impact sets in common drive sizes that pair well with cordless impacts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Owners commonly report that Tekton sockets fit fasteners well and hold up to regular use. Wall thickness is reasonable, though some premium brands use thinner walls for tighter clearances. For most automotive and household work, the difference is small, and the value advantage is large.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ratchets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Ratchets and Drive Tools
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton's ratchets are good everyday tools with fine-tooth mechanisms that reduce the swing arc needed to engage the next tooth. That makes them easier to use in tight spaces than older, coarse-tooth designs. Quick-release buttons, comfortable handles and reversible levers are standard.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where Tekton trails premium brands is in refinement. Ratchets from GearWrench, Snap-on and others often have smoother mechanisms, lower back-drag, and slimmer heads. For a home mechanic the difference rarely matters, but professionals who use a ratchet all day may notice it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Extensions, adapters and universal joints from Tekton are well made and fairly priced. Wobble extensions are handy for reaching bolts at slight angles. Like all drive tools, avoid using universal joints on torque-critical fasteners, since the angle and friction can throw off the reading.
          </p>

          <p>
            Breaker bars are worth owning alongside a ratchet. Breaking stubborn fasteners loose with a ratchet risks damaging the mechanism; a breaker bar does the job safely. Tekton's breaker bars are solid and inexpensive.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wrenches" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Wrenches, Pliers and Drivers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton combination wrenches are a standout value. They have clean finishes, consistent fit and complete size runs. The box ends grip well and the open ends are cut accurately. For most users they are hard to distinguish from wrenches that cost several times as much.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ratcheting wrenches are available and useful for jobs where swinging a ratchet is not possible. Tekton's ratcheting wrenches are good for the price, though premium brands may have finer mechanisms and thinner heads for very tight spots.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pliers and screwdrivers round out the lineup. Tekton's screwdrivers have comfortable handles and hardened tips, and its pliers cover common types such as slip-joint, needle-nose, locking and groove-joint. They are good everyday tools, though specialist brands like Knipex still lead in high-end pliers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hammers, pry bars, and other shop tools fill out the catalog. These are solid but not unique; buy them from Tekton for convenience if you are already ordering, but they are not the main reason to choose the brand.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="torque" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Tekton Torque Wrenches
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton's click torque wrenches are among its most popular products and a strong value. Its half-inch and three-eighths drive models are rated for about plus or minus 4 percent clockwise accuracy and ship with a calibration certificate. The 10 to 150 foot-pound half-inch model covers most passenger car lug nuts and brake and suspension work.
          </p>

          <p>
            These wrenches have distinct clicks, readable dual scales, and locking adjustment collars. They are the top picks for most home mechanics in our <a href="/best-torque-wrench/">best torque wrench guide</a>, where we explain how to choose the right range and keep a click wrench accurate over time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Precision instruments like torque wrenches are typically covered by a different, shorter warranty than Tekton's lifetime hand-tool coverage. Check current terms when you buy. Treat the wrench as a measuring tool: wind it down to its lowest setting after use, store it in its case and have it checked periodically.
          </p>

          <p>
            For truck owners who need more range, or professionals who use a torque wrench all day, a split beam or professional click wrench may be worth the extra money. For most car owners, Tekton's wrenches deliver more than enough accuracy at a modest price.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Tekton Torque Wrench Basics</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Plus or minus 4%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rated clockwise accuracy</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">10-150 ft-lb</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Range of the popular half-inch model</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">10-80 ft-lb</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Range of the three-eighths model</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Included</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Factory calibration certificate</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The Warranty in Practice
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tekton's hand tools carry a lifetime warranty, and the company is known for making claims simple. It generally does not require a receipt or registration for hand tools; contact customer service and it will replace or repair a broken tool. Owners commonly report that claims are handled quickly and without argument.
          </p>

          <p>
            That simplicity is a real advantage over some brands that require proof of purchase or a trip to a store. For online-only purchases, it means you do not need to keep track of a receipt for years to claim a replacement on a broken socket or wrench.
          </p>

          <p>
            Coverage is not universal. Precision measuring tools, consumables and some products have different terms. Normal wear on items like ratchet mechanisms may be handled through repair kits or replacement depending on the product. Read the current warranty on Tekton's site for details.
          </p>

          <p>
            Compared with truck brands, Tekton cannot swap a broken tool on the spot at your shop. Replacement is by mail, which can take several days. For a home mechanic that rarely matters; for a professional who depends on a tool daily, it is one reason to keep a backup.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="compared" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Tekton vs Other Brands
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Tekton vs GearWrench.</strong> GearWrench is Tekton's closest competitor and is sold widely in stores. GearWrench leads in ratchets and ratcheting wrenches, with refined mechanisms and a broad specialty range. Tekton often wins on complete sets, organizers and warranty simplicity. Many mechanics mix the two, buying GearWrench ratchets and Tekton sockets and wrenches.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Tekton vs Snap-on, Mac and Matco.</strong> The truck brands offer premium finishes, very thin walls, finer ratchets and on-site warranty service from a dealer who visits the shop. They also cost several times as much. For a professional who uses tools all day, that may be worth it for some tools. For home mechanics, Tekton delivers most of the function for a fraction of the cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Tekton vs Icon, Husky and Craftsman.</strong> Harbor Freight's Icon line competes on quality and price, and its stores make warranty exchanges easy. Husky and Craftsman are sold at big-box stores with straightforward warranties. Tekton tends to win on set completeness and organizers. Quality differences are small across this group; pick based on what fits your buying habits.
          </p>

          <p>
            The right choice depends on how you buy and use tools. If you like ordering online and building a kit from complete sets, Tekton is a natural fit. If you want to walk into a store for exchanges, Icon, Husky or Craftsman may suit you better.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What to Buy First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with a three-eighths drive socket set in both metric and SAE, with shallow and deep sockets, a ratchet, and extensions. That covers most automotive and household jobs. Add a set of combination wrenches in the same sizes so you can hold a nut while turning a bolt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next, add a half-inch drive set with a breaker bar for suspension and lug nuts, and a quarter-inch drive set for small fasteners. A half-inch click torque wrench is the most important precision tool to add for wheel and brake work.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then fill in with screwdrivers, pliers and a few specialty tools as projects demand. Tekton's modular storage makes it easy to keep everything organized as the kit grows.
          </p>

          <p>
            For tasks around the house beyond the garage, a good <a href="/best-stud-finder/">stud finder</a> and a <a href="/best-laser-level/">laser level</a> pair well with a hand-tool kit. With a solid set of Tekton hand tools, a cordless drill, and those measuring tools, most home projects become straightforward.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy complete sets once instead of loose pieces over time.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A complete socket and wrench set in metric and SAE costs less than buying individual pieces as needs arise, and you will never be stuck mid-job missing one size.</p>
          </div>
