        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using hand sockets on an impact wrench</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Chrome hand sockets are harder and more brittle than impact sockets. On an impact wrench they can crack or shatter, which is a safety risk. Use black chrome molybdenum impact sockets with any impact tool, and keep chrome sockets for hand ratchets and breaker bars.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Breaking bolts loose with a ratchet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ratchet mechanisms are not designed for the high force needed to free stubborn fasteners. Using one as a breaker bar can strip the pawl or break the head. Use a breaker bar for initial loosening, then switch to the ratchet to spin the fastener out.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying 12-point sockets for everyday use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Twelve-point sockets are useful for some fasteners and tight swing arcs, but they grip the corners of a hex and are more likely to round off stubborn or corroded bolts. For general automotive and household work, 6-point sockets are the safer choice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting truck-brand ratchets</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Tekton ratchets are good everyday tools, but they do not match the smoothness or slimness of premium brands. If you use a ratchet constantly, consider buying a premium ratchet and pairing it with Tekton sockets. That mix gives you the best value overall.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating a torque wrench like a ratchet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A torque wrench is a measuring tool. Do not use it to loosen bolts, drop it, or store it at a high setting. Wind a click wrench down to its lowest setting after use and keep it in its case. Treat it gently and it will stay accurate for years.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Tekton a good brand for a beginner mechanic?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Tekton's complete socket and wrench sets, readable markings and simple warranty make it an excellent starting point. A three-eighths drive set, combination wrenches and a click torque wrench cover most beginner jobs, from oil changes to brake work. The quality is high enough that you will not need to replace these tools as your skills grow.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Tekton need a receipt for warranty claims?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For hand tools, Tekton generally does not require a receipt or registration. Contact customer service with the broken tool and they will arrange a replacement. Precision instruments such as torque wrenches and some other products have different terms. Check Tekton's current warranty policy for the specific item before making a claim.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where are Tekton tools manufactured?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most Tekton tools are made in Taiwan and China, which is common for mid-priced hand tools. Taiwanese factories in particular produce tools for many well-known brands. Product listings usually state country of origin. If buying American-made tools is a priority, check each listing rather than assuming based on the brand.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do Tekton sockets compare with Snap-on?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Snap-on sockets often have thinner walls, finer finishes and on-site warranty service, and they cost several times as much. Tekton sockets fit well, use 6-point designs that resist rounding, and hold up to regular use. For home mechanics, Tekton delivers most of the function. For daily professional use, some prefer premium brands for specific tools.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a Tekton torque wrench accurate enough for wheels?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Tekton's click torque wrenches are rated at about plus or minus 4 percent clockwise and include a calibration certificate. That is accurate enough for lug nuts, brakes and suspension. Store the wrench at its lowest setting, handle it carefully, and have it checked every year or two to keep it reliable.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I buy Tekton tools in a store?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Tekton sells mainly online through its own website and Amazon. That keeps prices lower but means you cannot usually see the tools in person before buying. Read the listing details for sizes, drive and contents, and rely on the warranty if something arrives damaged or breaks later.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is GearWrench better than Tekton?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the tool. GearWrench is generally stronger in ratchets and ratcheting wrenches, and it has a broader specialty range sold in stores. Tekton often wins on complete sets, organizers and warranty simplicity. Many mechanics combine the two, using GearWrench ratchets with Tekton sockets and wrenches for the best overall value.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
